# Beam

> Beam provides CPU and GPU machines for agents, billed by the millisecond: persistent VMs that pause with their memory intact, sandboxes that boot in seconds, and GPU endpoints for inference.

> ## Agent Instructions
> Install the Beam CLI, sign in, and connect coding agents to the Beam MCP server with `curl -fsSL https://www.beam.cloud/install | sh`. To also create a VM, run `curl -fsSL https://www.beam.cloud/install | sh -s -- --vm`.
> VMs are persistent Linux machines without GPUs. Defaults: 4 CPUs, 8 GiB of RAM, a 50 GiB disk, Ubuntu 24.04, and a browser desktop. A running VM accrues usage until you stop, pause, or remove it, or until an idle timeout puts it to sleep.
> Stopping a VM keeps its disk and discards RAM and processes. Pausing keeps both, and resuming restores them. To pause after 5 idle minutes and wake on access, run `beam vm update NAME --ttl 300 --idle-action pause --auto-resume`.
> Core VM commands: `beam vm new NAME`, `beam vm ssh NAME`, `beam vm sync NAME ./dir`, `beam vm exec NAME -- CMD`, `beam vm expose NAME PORT --protected`, `beam vm fork NAME NEW_NAME`, `beam vm pause NAME`, `beam vm stop NAME`, `beam vm rm NAME`.
> For GPUs, use a sandbox or a deployed endpoint. Sandboxes are disposable environments that boot in seconds and fork from snapshots.
> Current rates are at https://www.beam.cloud/pricing.

- [Introduction](https://docs.beam.cloud/v2/getting-started/introduction.md): Beam provides CPU and GPU machines for agents, billed by the millisecond, including VMs that pause with their memory intact.
- [Give an Agent a Dev Box](https://docs.beam.cloud/v2/getting-started/quickstart-vm.md): Create a persistent VM, put a project on it, share a private URL, and let it sleep when idle
- [Run Agent Code in a Sandbox](https://docs.beam.cloud/v2/getting-started/quickstart-sandbox.md): Create a sandbox, run code, serve a URL, and fork it from a snapshot
- [Quickstart: Deploy an Endpoint](https://docs.beam.cloud/v2/getting-started/quickstart.md): Sign in, run a Python function, and deploy a web API
- [Set Up Coding Agents](https://docs.beam.cloud/v2/getting-started/add-to-cursor-claude.md): Connect your coding agent to Beam, then create its first VM
- [Installation](https://docs.beam.cloud/v2/getting-started/installation.md): Install the CLI, Python SDK, or TypeScript SDK and sign in
- [Authentication and Workspaces](https://docs.beam.cloud/v2/getting-started/authentication.md): Browser login, named contexts, and tokens for automation
- [Core Concepts](https://docs.beam.cloud/v2/getting-started/core-concepts.md)
- [Using the Dashboard](https://docs.beam.cloud/v2/getting-started/dashboard.md): Deploy projects and manage your workspace in the browser
